\section{Lag semantics}{

Lags are computed by time \strong{value}, not row position: \code{l(x, k)} at time
\code{t} is the value of \code{x} at time \code{t - k} within the same panel unit. If no
observation exists at \code{t - k} (start of the series, or a gap in the time
variable), the lag is \code{NA} and the row is dropped from the estimation
sample — exactly Stata's behavior. Lags never cross panel-unit boundaries.
The data does not need to be sorted.
}

\section{Differences from fixest}{

Following Stata (not fixest), \code{d(x, k)} is the k-th \strong{order} (iterated)
difference \verb{(1-L)^k x}, matching Stata's \code{Dk.x}; fixest's \code{d(x, k)} is the
distance-k difference \code{x - l(x, k)}. The two agree for \code{k = 1}.
}

\section{Usage constraints}{

Operators require \code{tvar} (and \code{ivar} for panel data) to be passed to
\code{\link[=ivreg2]{ivreg2()}}; \code{l()} and \code{d()} are not standalone lag utilities and signal an
error when called outside an \code{ivreg2()} formula (use, e.g.,
\code{dplyr::lag()} with a consecutive-periods check, or \code{fixest}/\code{collapse}/
\code{plm}, for general-purpose panel lagging). The lag/difference order \code{k}
must be a constant non-negative integer (or integer vector for ranges);
leads (negative \code{k}) and nested operators (\code{l(d(x))}) are not supported.
\code{predict(fit, newdata)} recomputes operator terms within \code{newdata}, so
\code{newdata} must contain the history rows needed for the lags; rows whose
lags are missing get \code{NA} predictions (again matching Stata).
}

